swap(x, y);

"output: 
x: bar
y: foo" c# also has a out keyword , and the difference between ref and out is a subtle one. from msdn: The caller of a method which takes an out parameter is not required to
  assign to the variable passed as the
  out parameter prior to the call;
  however, the callee is required to
  assign to the out parameter before
  returning. and In contrast ref parameters are considered initially assigned by the
  callee. As such, the callee is not
  required to assign to the ref parameter before use. Ref parameters
  are passed both into and out of a
  method. a small pitfall is, like in java, that objects passed by value can still be changed using their inner methods conclusion: c# passes its parameters, by default, by value but when needed parameters can also be passed by reference using the ref keyword inner methods from a parameter passed by value will alter the object (if that method itself alters some values) useful links: http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/vcsharp/aa336814.aspx http://www.c-sharpcorner.com/UploadFile/saragana/Willswapwork11162005012542AM/Willswapwork.aspx http://en.csharp-online.net/Value_vs_Reference

