I experienced a similar "Padding is invalid and cannot be removed." exception, but in my case the key IV and padding were correct. It turned out that flushing the crypto stream is all that was missing. Like this: MemoryStream msr3 = new MemoryStream();
            CryptoStream encStream = new CryptoStream(msr3, RijndaelAlg.CreateEncryptor(), CryptoStreamMode.Write);
            encStream.Write(bar2, 0, bar2.Length);
            // unless we flush the stream we would get "Padding is invalid and cannot be removed." exception when decoding
            encStream.FlushFinalBlock();
            byte[] bar3 = msr3.ToArray();

If you've ruled out key-mismatch, then besides FlushFinalBlock() (see Yaniv's answer), calling Close() on the CryptoStream will also suffice. If you are cleaning up resources strictly with using blocks, be sure to nest the block for the CryptoStream itself: using (MemoryStream ms = new MemoryStream())
using (var enc = RijndaelAlg.CreateEncryptor())
{
  using (CryptoStream encStream = new CryptoStream(ms, enc, CryptoStreamMode.Write))
  {
    encStream.Write(bar2, 0, bar2.Length);
  } // implicit close
  byte[] encArray = ms.ToArray();
} I've been bitten by this (or similar): using (MemoryStream ms = new MemoryStream())
using (var enc = RijndaelAlg.CreateEncryptor())
using (CryptoStream encStream = new CryptoStream(ms, enc, CryptoStreamMode.Write))
{
  encStream.Write(bar2, 0, bar2.Length);
  byte[] encArray = ms.ToArray();
} // implicit close -- too late!

